      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/as-pure-u/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Method A uses coordinate geometry. The tangent has gradient two, [B1] so 𝐶𝑇has gradient −1/2. [B1] Through 𝐶= (2, −3), 𝑦+ 3 = −1 2(𝑥−2), hence 2𝑦+ 𝑥+ 4 = 0. [M1] [A1] 𝑇 𝐶 Solve with 𝑦= 2𝑥−3. 2(2𝑥−3) + 𝑥+ 4 = 0, 5𝑥−2 = 0, so 𝑥= 2/5. [M1] Then 𝑦= 4/5 −3 = −11/5. [A1] The radius is the distance from 𝐶= (2, −3) to the point 𝑇= (2/5, −11/5). [B1] Apply the distance formula: [M1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/as-pure-u/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"√ 𝐶𝑇= √(2/5 −2)2 + (−11/5 + 3)2 = √64/25 + 16/25 = √16/5 = 4 [M1] [A1] 5/5 . Method B uses a repeated root. Substitute into (𝑥−2)2 + (𝑦+ 3)2 = 𝑟2. (𝑥−2)2 + (2𝑥)2 = 𝑟2, 5𝑥2 −4𝑥+ (4 −𝑟2) = 0. √ At tangency 16 −20(4 −𝑟2) = 0, so 20𝑟2 = 64, 𝑟2 = 16/5 and 𝑟= 4 5/5. Method C minimises by completing the square. A general point on the line is (𝑥, 2𝑥−3). Its squared distance from 𝐶is: 𝑑2 = (𝑥−2)2 + (2𝑥−3 + 3)2 = 5𝑥2 −4𝑥+ 4 = 5(𝑥−2/5)2 + 16/5. √ The minimum occurs at 𝑥= 2/5, giving 𝑟= 4 5/5. Each method independently establishes the radius.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
